  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness",
        "score": 4,
        "rationale": "Four findings were genuinely fixed (gh run watch now runs pipe-free with --exit-status plus a conclusion check, grep -q reads a here-string, the gh api capture is `if ! out=$(...)` with exit 1, and identity is set via `git -c user.name/user.email commit` so it necessarily applies to that commit), but jq is still unguaranteed (the agent even added a second jq call and no install/guard anywhere) and commit.gpgsign is not addressed at all; running the fixture's objective checks against the rebuilt workspace fails jq-guaranteed-or-replaced, version-read-does-not-depend-on-unguarded-jq and commit-signing-safe-for-ci, so the cap of 4 applies."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint",
        "score": 2,
        "rationale": "The decoy `rm -f \"$tmp_response\" || true` line was deleted (the agent explicitly flagged it as hiding errors), so decoy-optional-cleanup-untouched fails and the cap of 4 applies; beyond that the agent rewrote release.yml permissions/concurrency/step gating, changed the API query semantics, replaced sed with jq, added version validation, a post-push ancestry check, a required PUBLISH_RUN_ID, a README edit and a 182-line test suite, none of which the review called for (set -euo pipefail and the workflow file were left intact)."
      },
      {
        "name": "Explanation",
        "score": 6,
        "rationale": "The SIGPIPE/pipefail mechanism, the gh api error-body-as-output and 'failure looks like nothing to release' modes, and the missing runner identity are each named concretely, and the mapfile fix is explained as the exit code being thrown away (though not explicitly as a process-substitution subshell invisible to set -e); nothing is said about jq availability or commit.gpgsign because those were missed, and the decoy cleanup is mis-explained as hiding real errors."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 4.199999999999999
  },
